:name: EX-S04 - a retirement you did not order
:description: A valid MAC retired the operator's key. The operator did not send it.

# Execution profile (design 4.3; rules G1/G2 of section 9.2). FIRST, so a profile that cannot be
# found takes the machines down loudly instead of leaving every node running with no quantum set.
$profile?=@~/space-cs-sim/scripts/profiles/interactive.resc
include $profile

# Every path hangs off $out, which has NO default - see any other scenario in this repository for
# why. Unset, LoadELF fails where you can see it.
#
#   renode -e '$out=@'"$(make -s out)" -e 'include @<this file>' -e start
$comm?=$out/build-comm-s04-hard/zephyr/zephyr.elf
$obc?=$out/build-obc-u02-hard/zephyr/zephyr.elf
$eps?=$out/build-eps-hard/zephyr/zephyr.elf

# THERE IS NO ATTACKER MACHINE IN THIS SCENARIO, and the attacker is not on the crosslink.
#
# EX-S04's attacker is a PARTNER station: a second party that legitimately holds its own Security
# Association (SPI 11) on the same space link, the way cross-supported ground networks do. So it is
# not a compromised peer spacecraft - SDLS is a space-link protocol and the crosslink carries CSP,
# which EX-S01 already made the point of - it is another antenna on the same uplink, holding a real
# key. Both the partner and the operator are the host-side ground station, switching which SA it
# frames under, exactly as EX-S03's one station switched between the operator's two keys. What is on
# the wire is a valid MAC either way; what differs is WHOSE, and whether that entitles it to retire
# the operator's SA.
#
# Both halves carry all three associations and the STOP_SA directive handler. The difference is one
# authorisation check: may a frame authenticated under one SA retire an SA it does not own?
$comm1?=$out/build-comm-s01-sat1/zephyr/zephyr.elf

$comm0uart?=$out/s04-sat0-comm.uart
$obc0uart?=$out/s04-sat0-obc.uart
$eps0uart?=$out/s04-sat0-eps.uart
$comm1uart?=$out/s04-sat1-comm.uart
$linkport?=3777

emulation CreateCANHub "canHub0"
emulation CreateCANHub "canHub1"
emulation CreateCANHub "crosslinkHub"

# --- satellite 0: the victim. Everything hardened. ------------------------------------------------

mach create "SAT0_COMM"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platforms/boards/nucleo_h753zi.repl
connector Connect sysbus.fdcan1 canHub0
connector Connect sysbus.fdcan2 crosslinkHub
sysbus LoadELF $comm
sysbus.usart3 CreateFileBackend $comm0uart true
emulation CreateServerSocketTerminal $linkport "spacelink0" false
connector Connect sysbus.usart2 spacelink0
logLevel 3 sysbus
logLevel 3 rcc
logLevel 3 fdcan1
logLevel 3 fdcan2

mach create "SAT0_OBC"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platforms/boards/nucleo_h753zi.repl
connector Connect sysbus.fdcan1 canHub0
sysbus LoadELF $obc
sysbus.usart3 CreateFileBackend $obc0uart true
logLevel 3 sysbus
logLevel 3 rcc
logLevel 3 fdcan1

mach create "SAT0_EPS"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platforms/boards/nucleo_h753zi.repl
connector Connect sysbus.fdcan1 canHub0
sysbus LoadELF $eps
sysbus.usart3 CreateFileBackend $eps0uart true
logLevel 3 sysbus
logLevel 3 rcc
logLevel 3 fdcan1

# --- satellite 1: the neighbour, so the crosslink has a far end. ----------------------------------
#
# Present for the same reason it is in EX-S03, EX-L03 and EX-U01: this COMM image is built with the
# crosslink on, and a crosslink attached to nothing is a node logging a failure at startup for a
# reason that has nothing to do with the exercise. EX-S04's attacker is on the space link, not here.

mach create "SAT1_COMM"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platforms/boards/nucleo_h753zi.repl
connector Connect sysbus.fdcan1 canHub1
connector Connect sysbus.fdcan2 crosslinkHub
sysbus LoadELF $comm1
sysbus.usart3 CreateFileBackend $comm1uart true
logLevel 3 sysbus
logLevel 3 rcc
logLevel 3 fdcan1
logLevel 3 fdcan2
